cartridge "SPACE PATROL/COMPUTE!'S GAZETTE/1984"

' SPACE PATROL
' A ColecoVision game based on "Space Patrol", COMPUTE!'s Gazette July 1984
' (Commodore 64 and VIC-20; BASIC with a machine-language routine). Not the
' Ahoy! game of the same name (games/spatrol): a different game.
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: saucers drift down out of space, one row at a time, towards the
' landing pads on the ground. Your patrol ship stays in the middle of the
' screen: the stick moves it up and down, left and right turn it round
' and then fly it along the ground (the ground scrolls under you). The
' button fires a laser along your row, the way you face: it hits the first
' saucer in its path. Each shot costs a bomb from your load (the AMMO bar).
' With the bar empty, set down on a landing pad (fly the pad under the
' ship, go all the way down and hold down) to load up again. Every saucer
' that reaches the ground has landed: five landed saucers end the game.
' Points earn a bonus: after 500, then every 300 more, the supply ship
' takes one landed saucer away again.
'
' From the original (orig/space patrol_64.bas and its routines at $C000,
' disassembled in orig/ml.dis; run in jsc64): the ship fixed in the middle,
' 1 pixel a frame up and down; facing left or right, the first push only
' turning round; the ground strip with three pads scrolling (one cell
' about every 8 frames on the C64, 1 pixel a frame here); a laser that
' finds the first saucer in the ship's row on the facing side; levels 1-5
' with NL = 13 - level saucer slots, a game tick every 20 - 4 x level
' jiffies (+ the BASIC loop's time) picking one slot at random: an empty
' slot spawns a saucer at the top in a random column (never the ship's
' columns), a full one moves its saucer down a row if the row below is
' free; points per saucer 1 + 2L + int(L*L/2) (3, 7, 12, 17, 23); 20
' bombs, refuelling only with the load empty, the ship at the bottom over
' a pad and the stick held down; every refuel gives 2 + int(L/2) fewer
' bombs (never fewer than 5) and makes the tick 1 jiffy faster; the bonus
' at 500 and every 300 after it (a landed saucer back, or just the points
' when none has landed); five landed saucers end the game; the best score.
'
' Additions and liberties: saucers slide smoothly down (2 pixels a frame,
' a whole row per move) instead of jumping a row; the laser is a beam
' sprite crossing at 8 pixels a frame and the game keeps running (the
' C64 froze for the shot); the play field is 17 rows (the C64's 22), so a
' tick lasts 13/10 of the C64's to keep the same fall time; saucers keep a
' free row between them in a column (on the C64 a saucer that met another
' below it also counted as landed, an odd rule a player could not see);
' saucers only in 12 even columns (tile graphics); the laser hits a saucer
' whose body crosses the beam's line (pixel exact); a pad sets the ship
' down within 4 pixels of its middle; a bomb loads every 4 frames (the
' C64 froze while loading, with a blip a bomb); explosions, a supply ship rising for the bonus,
' a starfield; no pause (F1 on the C64: keypad not used); no play-again
' question; levels chosen with the stick.
'
' Controls: either controller. Stick up/down moves the ship, left/right
' turns and flies it along the ground, either side button fires. On the
' title, up/down choose, left/right set the level, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, the title tune
' played once. `set sprite count` always given. Own xorshift random
' numbers. Nothing flashes faster than 3 Hz.

